1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
You can fry it up, eat it for breakfast, or insulate your house with it.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
From the rice patties of Indonesia to the fertile fields of California,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
it's the workhorse of the grains.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Thousands of varieties and infinite dishes.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Rice is a wonderful invention.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
And guess who's the largest consumer of rice in the U.S.?

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Eat it, wait on it, wear it, live in it,

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,760
live in it, or turn it into something that can save millions

9
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,940
from starvation.

10
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,040
It's the world's most versatile grain,

11
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:36,940
rice on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,600
Rice is the world's food.

13
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:55,560
People get more calories from rice than from anything else.

14
00:00:55,561 --> 00:00:58,860
There are over 80,000 varieties.

15
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:00,200
This is a brown rice.

16
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,400
It's a pretty bulbous little grain.

17
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:05,400
This is a very popular rice, medium grain, white.

18
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:07,800
The reason it's white is it doesn't have the hull.

19
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:10,600
Black Thai rice, it's got an outer skin,

20
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,620
and then the inside is tender.

21
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:13,720
And it kind of cracks.

22
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:16,620
You snap in your teeth.

23
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,020
The rice restaurant in New York City

24
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:23,320
showcases the diversity of this amazing grain.

25
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:27,600
Its international menu pays homage to rice's worldwide reach.

26
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:31,260
Jamaican spiced jerk wings.

27
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:36,220
This is Korea through a bulgogi, a maki with bulgogi sauce.

28
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,860
It really opened my eyes up to the variety of rice

29
00:01:39,861 --> 00:01:40,820
that exists in the world.

30
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:47,220
Pad Thai is a rice noodle-based staple dish for Thailand.

31
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,460
There's a sauce that they put in it,

32
00:01:52,461 --> 00:01:54,360
and they mix it all together with the meatballs,

33
00:01:54,361 --> 00:01:55,660
and it makes it really tasty.

34
00:01:55,661 --> 00:01:56,760
The flavor is really good.

35
00:01:56,761 --> 00:01:58,060
It's with garlic.

36
00:01:58,061 --> 00:01:59,960
It's the grain of 100,000 dishes.

37
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:07,380
It was first cultivated 10,000 years ago in China,

38
00:02:07,381 --> 00:02:11,080
and today it's the most important grain in the world.

39
00:02:11,081 --> 00:02:15,420
Half of humanity eats it for breakfast, lunch, and dinner.

40
00:02:15,421 --> 00:02:18,780
Most of the world's rice is in Asia, 90% of it.

41
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:22,160
Here in the US, our consumption is rather modest,

42
00:02:22,161 --> 00:02:24,660
25 pounds per person per year.

43
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:26,400
But there are a lot of places in Asia

44
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:30,900
where people are consuming 200 pounds per year.

45
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,800
It's cultivated on every continent

46
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:38,920
except Antarctica, on farms from small to mammoth,

47
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:42,320
from subsistence farmers feeding their families

48
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:47,020
to industrial farmers shipping product around the world.

49
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,020
In Asia, the vast majority of their 200 million rice farms

50
00:02:51,021 --> 00:02:53,600
still use the centuries-old techniques.

51
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:00,260
The way the U.S. rice industry is competitive

52
00:03:00,261 --> 00:03:03,480
is by highly mechanizing the crop.

53
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,620
It takes about 10 man-hours per acre

54
00:03:05,621 --> 00:03:07,260
to grow a rice crop here in the U.S.

55
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:08,880
Some of those places in Asia

56
00:03:08,881 --> 00:03:11,600
is taking 200 and 300 man-hours per acre.

57
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:14,840
Labor's got to be cheap for that to work.

58
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:19,000
The majority of the world's rice

59
00:03:19,001 --> 00:03:20,220
is planted by hand.

60
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:24,780
But in the United States, the seed is usually spread by airplane.

61
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:31,400
The most popular rice brand in America is Uncle Ben's.

62
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:34,300
At their production facility in Greenville, Mississippi,

63
00:03:34,301 --> 00:03:38,940
they churn out a million pounds of packaged rice every 24 hours.

64
00:03:38,941 --> 00:03:41,480
That's enough to feed a city the size of New York

65
00:03:41,481 --> 00:03:44,720
everyone for its aid.

66
00:03:44,721 --> 00:03:46,840
All to be served up in less than 10 minutes

67
00:03:46,841 --> 00:03:47,920
of convenient cooking time.

68
00:03:47,921 --> 00:03:54,920
How does rice get from this to this?

69
00:03:54,921 --> 00:03:59,920
The rice comes into Uncle Ben's fresh off the field, husks and all.

70
00:03:59,921 --> 00:04:05,920
It's stored in rough rice silos until they need it for production.

71
00:04:05,921 --> 00:04:10,920
From the silos, it goes straight into the steamer to be parvoiled.

72
00:04:10,921 --> 00:04:14,920
The steamer back here behind me is running about 30 tons an hour,

73
00:04:14,921 --> 00:04:21,920
and it's operating at a temperature of about 230 to 240 degrees Fahrenheit.

74
00:04:21,921 --> 00:04:25,920
Parboiling simply means partially boiled,

75
00:04:25,921 --> 00:04:30,920
but the effect on the rice grain is much more complicated than it sounds.

76
00:04:30,921 --> 00:04:35,920
The rice grains enter the steamer with their inedible husks still on.

77
00:04:35,921 --> 00:04:41,920
The steaming forces B vitamins and minerals from the outer layers into the starchy center.

78
00:04:41,921 --> 00:04:46,920
Later, the husk will be removed and the bran layer milled off.

79
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:50,160
The parboiling results in a milled white rice grain with

80
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:53,160
close to the same nutritional value as a whole grain brown

81
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:53,960
rice.

82
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:58,980
After the parboiling, the rice will

83
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:02,380
go through a multi-layer drying process.

84
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:06,380
Six hours later, it's ready for the mill, where first the husk

85
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:08,720
and then the bran layer are removed.

86
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:10,820
This is the final step of milling.

87
00:05:10,821 --> 00:05:11,521
milling.

88
00:05:11,522 --> 00:05:15,540
What you're going to see here is the grain actually being removed from the rice.

89
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:19,045
That's the grain falling off.

90
00:05:19,046 --> 00:05:22,260
So you're really fine-tuning your milling at this point.

91
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:28,180
After milling, they have to make sure that all the rice grains are uniform for the best quality

92
00:05:28,181 --> 00:05:32,980
product. So every one of them has to pass through a ScanMaster.

93
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:38,660
This machine's job is to find the discolored grains.

94
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:47,820
Each grain flies individually by a camera.

95
00:05:47,821 --> 00:05:52,820
A beam of light bounced off the grain

96
00:05:52,821 --> 00:05:54,820
delivers color data to a computer.

97
00:05:54,821 --> 00:05:59,820
Any deviation from the standard triggers swift action.

98
00:05:59,821 --> 00:06:06,820
A pump of air knocks the flawed grain from the flow.

99
00:06:06,821 --> 00:06:09,780
from the flow.

100
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:14,060
It all happens in a split second.

101
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:16,360
32,000 pounds of rice an hour.

102
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:26,180
11,734,000,000 individual grains evaluated every minute.

103
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:35,920
But the quality control needs quality control.

104
00:06:35,921 --> 00:06:38,860
Samples of rice from the Scan Master go to Kenny Winters

105
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:40,560
to be evaluated.

106
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:42,420
When the rice goes through my eyes,

107
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:46,760
I'm saying that it's ready for consumption.

108
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:50,560
So the last pair of eyes, I'm the last one to see this rice

109
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:53,980
before it goes into your cleat.

110
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,780
If Kenny sees too many grains that don't measure up,

111
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:59,120
he'll adjust the Scan Master's sensitivity

112
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:01,820
to knock out more imperfect grains.

113
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:05,920
The end result should look like this, not like this.

114
00:07:05,921 --> 00:07:11,920
Sifters remove any last bits of debris or broken grains.

115
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:13,920
Then it's moved to the finished rice bin

116
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:15,920
to wait for the next step.

117
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:22,880
In this room, we have six packaging lines.

118
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:25,880
This particular line right here is making a

119
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:28,880
boil-in-the-bag type product.

120
00:07:28,980 --> 00:07:30,880
Today, it's a brown rice.

121
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:35,920
The difference between brown rice and brown rice

122
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:39,580
and white rice is simply whether or not the bran layer is milled off.

123
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:47,540
Usually regular white rice is much less nutritious than brown rice.

124
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:52,920
But with the parboiling process, the white rice is made nearly as nutritious as the brown.

125
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:19,040
Before the finished product goes out on the shipping pallets,

126
00:08:19,041 --> 00:08:23,680
the production run has to satisfy the pallets of the sensory panel.

127
00:08:23,681 --> 00:08:34,225
Make sure it's not too salty. Moisture level's right. Make sure the taste is proper.

128
00:08:34,226 --> 00:08:35,480
Very good.

129
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:38,720
That's how they make it perfect every time.

130
00:08:38,721 --> 00:08:43,720
But it's also perfect for something else, other than eating.

131
00:08:43,721 --> 00:08:45,720
Uncle Ben's is the best.

132
00:08:45,721 --> 00:08:48,720
I have tried many different kinds throughout the years,

133
00:08:48,721 --> 00:08:52,720
the last 16 years, but nothing is as good as Uncle Ben's.

134
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:57,720
It doesn't crack and lasts forever.

135
00:08:57,721 --> 00:09:02,720
Vivian Robinson writes on Uncle Ben's rice.

136
00:09:02,721 --> 00:09:05,720
For a donation, she will write your name on a single grain.

137
00:09:05,721 --> 00:09:09,720
We're at Venice Beach, the best place on earth.

138
00:09:09,721 --> 00:09:11,720
Paradise, in my opinion.

139
00:09:11,721 --> 00:09:15,720
The whole world comes down here, so you can't beat Venice.

140
00:09:15,721 --> 00:09:18,720
Get your name on a grain of rice.

141
00:09:18,721 --> 00:09:22,720
It goes on a necklace, keychain, cell phone charm, bracelet.

142
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:29,720
It takes a lot of patience, steady hands.

143
00:09:29,721 --> 00:09:31,720
I never drink coffee.

144
00:09:31,721 --> 00:09:34,720
Try not to shake at all.

145
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:38,720
Maybe though Venice Beach has a lot of distractions.

146
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:42,420
I'm kind of used to it.

147
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:46,460
It can be about usually 12 letters.

148
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:48,960
One guy asked me, can you write Rumpelstiltskin

149
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:50,160
on a grain of rice?

150
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:51,860
So I said, sure.

151
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,780
Sure enough, 14 letters, I got it on there.

152
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:58,680
Sometimes I amaze myself what I can do.

153
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:03,480
She draws pictures on request, or will gladly write in Korean

154
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:04,620
or any language you want.

155
00:10:04,621 --> 00:10:05,620
you want.

156
00:10:05,621 --> 00:10:06,620
It's funny.

157
00:10:06,621 --> 00:10:07,620
Oh, really?

158
00:10:07,621 --> 00:10:08,620
It's so small.

159
00:10:08,621 --> 00:10:10,620
It's real small.

160
00:10:10,621 --> 00:10:13,620
16 years ago when I was on vacation in Portugal,

161
00:10:13,621 --> 00:10:16,620
I met a guy from India who told me,

162
00:10:16,621 --> 00:10:18,620
I couldn't believe he was doing it, and he told me,

163
00:10:18,621 --> 00:10:20,620
oh, you should do this in America.

164
00:10:20,621 --> 00:10:21,620
They'll love it.

165
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:24,620
I go, well, seems like an odd thing to do,

166
00:10:24,621 --> 00:10:26,620
but let me give it a try.

167
00:10:26,621 --> 00:10:30,620
So 16 years later, I'm still doing it.

168
00:10:30,621 --> 00:10:35,620
She puts the grains in little glass tubes filled with oil

169
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:38,560
to preserve the grain and magnify it.

170
00:10:38,660 --> 00:10:43,060
Now I'm going to seal it and put it on a necklace for you.

171
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:44,560
That's how I write on rice.

172
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:51,180
Rice is a centuries-old symbol of good luck,

173
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,880
and it's easy to see why.

174
00:10:53,980 --> 00:10:57,680
A full bowl of rice is a fortunate thing.

175
00:10:57,681 --> 00:11:02,680
Next, the highly mechanized process of growing rice in America.

176
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,680
Rice is thrown at weddings for good luck,

177
00:11:06,681 --> 00:11:09,680
but some people say it's not good luck for the birds that eat it,

178
00:11:09,681 --> 00:11:13,680
that it will expand in their stomachs and cause them to explode.

179
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:18,680
But migratory birds gobble up the leftover grain on rice farms

180
00:11:18,681 --> 00:11:19,680
with no ill effects.

181
00:11:19,681 --> 00:11:21,680
So exploding birds?

182
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:22,680
Just a minute.

183
00:11:22,681 --> 00:11:26,580
in just a minute.

184
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:29,820
Rice is a grass, like corn and wheat,

185
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:31,380
but it's much more important.

186
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:34,020
It feeds more people.

187
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:36,900
It was first cultivated 10,000 years ago on the banks

188
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,500
of the Yangtze River.

189
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:43,400
And since then, humans have bred it into 80,000 varieties

190
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:46,700
and spread it all over the world.

191
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,540
When it's soaked in vinegar, the Japanese call it sushi.

192
00:11:50,541 --> 00:11:54,340
Sushi has roots 1,700 years old, but at first,

193
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:56,740
it didn't look anything like this.

194
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:00,380
The rice was just a preservative.

195
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:03,580
Rice was packed around raw fish, and the fermenting grain

196
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:07,300
would preserve the fish for months.

197
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:10,700
Then the rice was thrown away.

198
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:14,200
Today, sushi is popular in America, too.

199
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,600
Virtually all the sushi rice in the US

200
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:17,600
is grown in California.

201
00:12:20,540 --> 00:12:24,280
This 3,000-acre farm in the Sacramento Valley

202
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:28,480
grows premium-quality sushi rice.

203
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:32,280
The part we eat is the white, starchy center of the seed.

204
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:34,780
If the nutrient-rich bran layer is still on,

205
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:37,600
it's called brown rice.

206
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:40,500
It's all encased in the tough outer husk.

207
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:47,040
This rice isn't ready to harvest yet, but it will be soon.

208
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:50,540
It's time to prep the fields.

209
00:12:50,541 --> 00:12:53,180
The rice is in several inches of standing water,

210
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:55,640
which would make it hard for the harvesters.

211
00:12:55,740 --> 00:12:59,020
So first, they have to drain the fields.

212
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:04,580
For that, they need a unique piece of equipment, the DASIG.

213
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:08,420
We need to get some increased drainage of those fields.

214
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:10,320
Otherwise, it would take far too long

215
00:13:10,420 --> 00:13:13,260
to try to get the field to drain.

216
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:15,700
The DASIG has a very special task

217
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:19,360
for its heavy, sharp wheels.

218
00:13:19,361 --> 00:13:21,860
They'll cut through the dirt levees that hold the water

219
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:22,760
on the field.

220
00:13:26,660 --> 00:13:28,800
When the daisy gets to one of the levees that encircle

221
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:31,300
the field, it slices right through it.

222
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:44,620
The wheels cut deep burrows that lead the water off.

223
00:13:44,621 --> 00:13:48,820
off.

224
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:51,100
Four to six inches of water has been on this field

225
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:54,360
since it was planted.

226
00:13:54,460 --> 00:13:57,300
It may seem like a lot, but 40% of it goes back

227
00:13:57,400 --> 00:13:59,700
into the system through surface or groundwater.

228
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:09,840
The furrows made by the DESIG lead the water

229
00:14:09,940 --> 00:14:11,020
to the network of drains.

230
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:21,260
So it'll drain out so there's nothing.

231
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,760
It'll just become mud and then eventually dry up

232
00:14:24,860 --> 00:14:28,560
so that it's dry enough for the harvester to get in.

233
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:30,400
With the water off the field,

234
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:32,500
the rice is more vulnerable.

235
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:34,680
Now it's a waiting game.

236
00:14:34,780 --> 00:14:39,040
A bit of bad weather can really make or break you.

237
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:40,540
So you're anxious.

238
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:41,940
We're all anxious.

239
00:14:41,941 --> 00:14:43,200
So you're anxious.

240
00:14:43,300 --> 00:14:47,180
We're all anxious to get this rice cut and in the barn,

241
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:50,620
so to speak, or in our rice drying and storage facility

242
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:54,460
before bad weather hits it.

243
00:14:54,560 --> 00:14:56,980
In 30 days, the rice will be dried out.

244
00:14:57,080 --> 00:14:59,160
Harvesting will have to happen soon.

245
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:02,320
The rice is five months old now and at the peak of ripeness.

246
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:06,960
The harvesters are ready to go.

247
00:15:06,961 --> 00:15:13,960
five at once take on this 150-acre field.

248
00:15:13,961 --> 00:15:27,961
400 reel teeth comb the stalks across the cutters.

249
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:32,960
This variety has to be cut close to the ground,

250
00:15:32,961 --> 00:15:35,960
which is more challenging for the harvesters.

251
00:15:35,961 --> 00:15:38,200
It's hard to harvest.

252
00:15:38,201 --> 00:15:39,460
It can be lying down.

253
00:15:39,461 --> 00:15:43,060
It'll fall down flat where medium grain will stay standing.

254
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:45,800
And it is a lower-yielding product.

255
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:48,520
And so we do have to achieve a higher price

256
00:15:48,521 --> 00:15:51,080
to make it all work at the end of the day.

257
00:15:57,760 --> 00:15:59,620
The plants fall onto a conveyor

258
00:15:59,621 --> 00:16:01,720
that carries them inside the body of the harvester.

259
00:16:01,721 --> 00:16:08,720
Once inside, the crop is carried to the chaper, which shakes the rice grains loose of the stalks.

260
00:16:08,721 --> 00:16:12,720
The grains fall through multiple sieves into a collecting tank.

261
00:16:12,721 --> 00:16:15,720
An auger spirals the grain up into the holding tank.

262
00:16:15,721 --> 00:16:24,720
Meanwhile, high-volume air blows the chaff away from the grain toward the back of the machine, where it is ejected.

263
00:16:24,721 --> 00:16:28,720
A fan spreads it evenly.

264
00:16:28,721 --> 00:16:30,760
evenly.

265
00:16:30,860 --> 00:16:33,960
After harvesting, the field will be flooded again,

266
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:36,460
and this straw will rot over the winter,

267
00:16:36,560 --> 00:16:38,660
making a rich fertilizer for next year's crop.

268
00:16:42,760 --> 00:16:45,680
This is where we just get to witness the results of all

269
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:48,680
the hard work.

270
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:51,980
The machine has a tremendous amount of instrumentation

271
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:55,980
and a very highly technical piece of equipment.

272
00:16:56,080 --> 00:16:58,220
You know, auto steering is a feature that

273
00:16:58,221 --> 00:17:01,060
that can be added to a combine where this thing will drive

274
00:17:01,061 --> 00:17:05,900
itself and it'll make perfectly straight passes.

275
00:17:05,901 --> 00:17:07,760
If I had that feature in here, I don't really

276
00:17:07,761 --> 00:17:08,800
know what I would do.

277
00:17:08,801 --> 00:17:11,340
I mean, I kind of get a little bit of enjoyment

278
00:17:11,341 --> 00:17:13,460
out of having to drive the machine.

279
00:17:13,461 --> 00:17:15,900
That feature is something I've never really desired.

280
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:27,020
The holding tank fills up in about 30 minutes.

281
00:17:27,021 --> 00:17:29,660
pull up alongside to collect the grain

282
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:33,020
and take it back to the storage facility.

283
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:35,260
People all over the world eat rice,

284
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:37,760
and the more and more efficient we can be,

285
00:17:37,860 --> 00:17:40,260
the more important it is because, as you know,

286
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:43,260
it's hard to grow more farmland throughout the world.

287
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:45,980
Really, we're best if we can be more efficient

288
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:48,280
on each and every acre that we have

289
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:53,020
so we can produce more rice to help feed the world.

290
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:55,720
All over California, rice farms

291
00:17:55,721 --> 00:17:59,280
rice farms are helping to feed the world in all its variety.

292
00:18:00,340 --> 00:18:04,160
Next, rice for snacking or for drinking.

293
00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:12,480
As a tourist attraction, some Japanese villages

294
00:18:12,481 --> 00:18:14,660
create works of art on patty meals

295
00:18:14,661 --> 00:18:17,160
by planting different colored rice varieties.

296
00:18:20,220 --> 00:18:23,700
It feeds millions, but it's also a great snack.

297
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:25,220
It's rice.

298
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:28,960
Rice Krispie treats, rice cakes, and the cereal that

299
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:30,160
talks to you.

300
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:31,060
They're Brussels.

301
00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:32,560
Rice Krispies.

302
00:18:32,660 --> 00:18:35,760
Food scientists have actually studied why Rice Krispies make

303
00:18:35,860 --> 00:18:36,960
their characteristic sound.

304
00:18:40,260 --> 00:18:43,380
The answer is in the structure of each crispy.

305
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:46,700
Sugar and rice powder are heated to such a high temperature

306
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:51,040
that the sugar and starch bond into a glass-like substance.

307
00:18:51,140 --> 00:18:54,180
As they expand, air bubbles form.

308
00:18:54,181 --> 00:18:58,400
The dried crispies are loaded with big air pockets with super thin walls.

309
00:18:59,920 --> 00:19:03,920
When milk is poured on, it fills the cavities and squeezes the air.

310
00:19:04,980 --> 00:19:06,980
The walls shatter like glass.

311
00:19:07,420 --> 00:19:08,740
Snap, crackle, pop.

312
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:12,360
Pop rocks are the only other food that behaves like this.

313
00:19:15,620 --> 00:19:18,140
Here's another American favorite, Quaker.

314
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:19,900
They make more than oats.

315
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:24,680
The company has used a Quaker man as its logo since 1877,

316
00:19:25,020 --> 00:19:26,900
but not due to any Quaker connection.

317
00:19:27,620 --> 00:19:29,800
The founder just read about them in the encyclopedia.

318
00:19:32,780 --> 00:19:34,560
To make their puffed rice cake,

319
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:37,140
they start with whole grain brown rice.

320
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:41,340
They go through 50 of these super sacks a day,

321
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:44,640
a total of 100,000 pounds of rice.

322
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:49,660
The first hopper starts the process

323
00:19:49,661 --> 00:19:52,600
of getting the rice puff ready.

324
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:54,400
We're adding steam and water to make sure

325
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:58,160
we get the right moisture of the grain.

326
00:19:58,260 --> 00:20:01,100
And then from here, we're going to take the product, once it's

327
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:03,500
right, and we're going to set it into our popping area,

328
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:06,140
where we're going to actually pop the product.

329
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:08,920
That part of the process is a trade secret.

330
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:11,280
They wouldn't let us film the equipment.

331
00:20:11,380 --> 00:20:14,880
But we know it works something like this.

332
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:18,620
The process is a little different from how popcorn is popped.

333
00:20:18,621 --> 00:20:21,620
Popping corn has a tougher bran layer that keeps the grain

334
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:26,260
under pressure until the pressure gets too great.

335
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:29,160
But popping rice is more difficult.

336
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:31,600
A precise quantity of raw, wet rice

337
00:20:31,700 --> 00:20:34,060
goes into cake-shaped molds.

338
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:37,380
The mold keeps the grain under pressure while it's heated.

339
00:20:37,480 --> 00:20:39,000
The water inside the grain starts

340
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:41,940
to turn into steam and expand.

341
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:45,880
After about 10 seconds, the mold releases the pressure suddenly,

342
00:20:45,980 --> 00:20:47,440
and the grains puff out.

343
00:20:47,441 --> 00:20:52,780
The shape of the dish packs the puffs tightly together.

344
00:20:52,880 --> 00:20:55,380
The whole process only takes about 15 seconds.

345
00:20:58,380 --> 00:21:00,680
They come out of the popper piping hot.

346
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:06,000
Right now, they're nothing but rice.

347
00:21:06,100 --> 00:21:08,000
Flavorings would impede the puff,

348
00:21:08,100 --> 00:21:11,140
so they have to be sprayed on later.

349
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:14,240
We make about 2,000 rice cakes a minute,

350
00:21:14,340 --> 00:21:17,440
about 3 million rice cakes a day.

351
00:21:17,441 --> 00:21:31,441
The rice cakes are spread out.

352
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:47,240
They have to be in a single layer for the flavorings to be sprayed on the surface.

353
00:21:47,241 --> 00:21:50,400
are baked to reduce the moisture level and add texture.

354
00:21:56,880 --> 00:21:58,780
Next, it's on to quality control.

355
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:05,200
The cakes whiz by a laser and a camera, which

356
00:22:05,201 --> 00:22:06,200
registers their position.

357
00:22:08,900 --> 00:22:11,540
If the shape is wrong, then less than a second later,

358
00:22:11,541 --> 00:22:14,600
when it reaches this gap, a nozzle

359
00:22:14,601 --> 00:22:16,200
into position for interception.

360
00:22:19,200 --> 00:22:21,600
A blast of compressed air knocks it out of the lineup.

361
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:30,840
Rice cakes may be a recent snack food,

362
00:22:30,940 --> 00:22:34,160
but they have long historical roots.

363
00:22:34,260 --> 00:22:36,560
Every culture that traditionally eats rice

364
00:22:36,660 --> 00:22:38,060
is something called a rice cake.

365
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:43,900
The Indian idli, the Filipino puto,

366
00:22:43,901 --> 00:22:47,500
The Japanese Moki, the Indonesian Keta Pot,

367
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:50,800
the Chinese Nian Gao are just a few examples.

368
00:22:53,840 --> 00:22:57,340
For the 3 billion people that eat rice three times a day,

369
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:59,540
a rice cake is just the natural thing to do.

370
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:07,800
Quaker rice cakes are made with whole grain brown rice,

371
00:23:07,900 --> 00:23:09,900
which means the bran layer isn't milled off.

372
00:23:09,901 --> 00:23:15,800
But in the US, most of the market for rice is white rice.

373
00:23:15,900 --> 00:23:20,840
Almost any variety can be milled down to make white rice.

374
00:23:27,360 --> 00:23:30,760
In California's Rice Valley, a shipment of grain fresh off

375
00:23:30,860 --> 00:23:34,700
the field is arriving at the Farmer's Rice Cooperative,

376
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:36,800
where the grain will be milled and cleaned.

377
00:23:39,900 --> 00:23:43,940
Most of it comes in as paddy rice, the whole seed with the

378
00:23:44,040 --> 00:23:46,040
inedible husk still on.

379
00:23:46,140 --> 00:23:49,940
The rice is deposited into the pit from the truck.

380
00:23:50,040 --> 00:23:52,440
It then goes to our storage facility.

381
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:55,440
Prior to going into the silos, it goes through a rough cleaning

382
00:23:55,540 --> 00:23:56,660
process.

383
00:23:56,760 --> 00:24:00,360
It is cleaned of straw and other large pieces of material.

384
00:24:02,460 --> 00:24:05,200
The final part of our cleaning process is our destoning

385
00:24:05,300 --> 00:24:06,400
process.

386
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:09,200
That's where the rice runs across a destoner that

387
00:24:09,201 --> 00:24:11,700
in the fluidized bed.

388
00:24:11,800 --> 00:24:14,400
On the fluidized bed, air blows the grains away

389
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:19,240
from the heavier pebbles, which get carried out the back.

390
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:22,540
California farms produce more than 4 billion pounds of rice

391
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:26,260
a year, and about a quarter of it comes through here.

392
00:24:26,360 --> 00:24:28,660
Basically, it's a lot of rice.

393
00:24:28,760 --> 00:24:30,760
If we combine both of our two mills,

394
00:24:30,860 --> 00:24:35,260
we have the largest processing facility in the US.

395
00:24:35,360 --> 00:24:37,800
Spiral conveyors move the rice through tubes

396
00:24:37,801 --> 00:24:40,640
to the next step, the huller.

397
00:24:40,740 --> 00:24:42,900
The inedible husk is removed by passing it

398
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:44,740
through spinning cylinders.

399
00:24:44,840 --> 00:24:47,240
The rice falls between the rubber rolls,

400
00:24:47,340 --> 00:24:51,140
and the husk is sheared off.

401
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:52,940
The husks get used for everything,

402
00:24:53,040 --> 00:24:55,960
from insulation to a fuel source to pillow stuffing.

403
00:25:00,960 --> 00:25:03,000
Most of their customers want white rice,

404
00:25:03,100 --> 00:25:05,500
so next it goes to milling.

405
00:25:05,501 --> 00:25:11,740
They process 1,200 tons of paddy rice a day, 48 truckloads.

406
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:15,740
We still just outfill one of these silos in a day.

407
00:25:15,840 --> 00:25:18,740
Next door, packaging, from two-pound bags

408
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:19,740
to railroad cars.

409
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:28,960
Today, they're packing 20-pound bags of white rice.

410
00:25:35,500 --> 00:25:38,500
White rice will store much longer than brown rice,

411
00:25:38,501 --> 00:25:41,500
but most of the nutrients were removed with the bran layer.

412
00:25:41,501 --> 00:25:44,500
If the customer wants it enriched with B vitamins and iron,

413
00:25:44,501 --> 00:25:46,500
the mill can do that too.

414
00:25:46,501 --> 00:25:49,500
It's sprayed on the grain surface.

415
00:25:49,501 --> 00:25:52,500
That's why they say not to wash white rice.

416
00:25:52,501 --> 00:25:55,500
The water will rinse off the nutrients.

417
00:25:55,501 --> 00:26:02,500
The water will rinse off the nutrients.

418
00:26:04,740 --> 00:26:09,500
It goes through a checkweigher to make sure the right amount of rice is in the bag.

419
00:26:09,501 --> 00:26:14,200
It goes through a metal detector to make sure there are no metal contaminants in the bag.

420
00:26:14,201 --> 00:26:20,200
Then it moves to our palletizer.

421
00:26:20,201 --> 00:26:27,200
The palletizer can pack a pallet in five minutes flat and never needs a coffee break.

422
00:26:27,201 --> 00:26:34,200
About half the rice is sold on the international market.

423
00:26:34,201 --> 00:26:39,200
Wrap it up, ship it out.

424
00:26:39,201 --> 00:26:49,200
Their domestic customers are mostly food manufacturers who will turn plain rice into everything from side dishes to meals to snacks for the amount of food.

425
00:26:49,201 --> 00:26:51,040
to snacks for the American consumer.

426
00:26:54,740 --> 00:26:56,780
Weiss is unique among the major grains.

427
00:26:58,820 --> 00:27:00,820
Most of it gets eaten as a cooked grain,

428
00:27:01,140 --> 00:27:03,940
but we also ground it into flour.

429
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:08,080
It even gets fermented into alcohol, like sake.

430
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:11,380
The word sake doesn't exist.

431
00:27:11,620 --> 00:27:13,120
Everything's a S-A-K-I.

432
00:27:13,240 --> 00:27:14,120
It's not. It's actually sake.

433
00:27:14,560 --> 00:27:17,020
And I tell people, just kind of slur your words, sake.

434
00:27:17,460 --> 00:27:19,160
So the more you drink, the better you are.

435
00:27:19,200 --> 00:27:21,960
pronouncing sake.

436
00:27:21,961 --> 00:27:25,940
The word actually means anti-alcoholic drink.

437
00:27:25,941 --> 00:27:30,380
What we call sake, the Japanese call nihonshu.

438
00:27:30,381 --> 00:27:33,140
But whatever you call it, it's an ancient beverage

439
00:27:33,141 --> 00:27:35,480
on a modern course.

440
00:27:35,481 --> 00:27:39,580
Bo Timken opened the first US store totally devoted to sake.

441
00:27:39,581 --> 00:27:43,960
He loves sake, but in the beginning, he had much to learn.

442
00:27:43,961 --> 00:27:45,860
In my progression of understanding

443
00:27:45,861 --> 00:27:48,200
and trying to understand, I had to knock down all these pegs

444
00:27:48,201 --> 00:27:50,140
of everything that I thought sake was.

445
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:51,980
I thought it was a distilled beverage.

446
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:52,940
It's not.

447
00:27:53,080 --> 00:27:54,300
I thought you had to drink it hot.

448
00:27:54,460 --> 00:27:55,160
You don't.

449
00:27:55,220 --> 00:27:56,940
I thought you had to drink it out of little shot cups.

450
00:27:57,220 --> 00:27:57,920
You don't.

451
00:27:57,921 --> 00:27:59,820
I thought you had to slug it like a booze.

452
00:28:00,500 --> 00:28:01,200
You don't.

453
00:28:01,320 --> 00:28:03,120
I thought you could only eat sushi with it.

454
00:28:03,340 --> 00:28:04,440
There are all these misconceptions.

455
00:28:04,620 --> 00:28:08,020
I thought, jeez, I can't be the only guy that just doesn't get it.

456
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:12,840
His store sells more than 150 brands of sake,

457
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:14,800
even one with real gold in it.

458
00:28:14,801 --> 00:28:21,800
From $150 a bottle down to $6.

459
00:28:21,801 --> 00:28:25,640
It's a 1,000-year-old beverage steeped in tradition,

460
00:28:25,641 --> 00:28:27,640
but sake brewers are trying to attract

461
00:28:27,641 --> 00:28:29,640
the younger generation as well.

462
00:28:29,641 --> 00:28:33,560
So these are not your grandfather's sakes?

463
00:28:33,561 --> 00:28:36,560
Sparkling sakes, fruit-flavored sakes,

464
00:28:36,561 --> 00:28:39,800
unfiltered sakes, low-alcohol sakes.

465
00:28:39,801 --> 00:28:42,800
Sake has come a long way.

466
00:28:42,801 --> 00:28:46,640
It's been brewed in Japan for more than 1,800 years.

467
00:28:46,740 --> 00:28:48,640
At its core, it's simple.

468
00:28:48,740 --> 00:28:51,040
You can take steamed rice, put it in your mouth,

469
00:28:51,140 --> 00:28:52,440
chew it up, spit in a bucket.

470
00:28:52,540 --> 00:28:55,440
The enzymes in your mouth would break that long-chain starch

471
00:28:55,540 --> 00:28:57,240
molecule into a glucose.

472
00:28:57,340 --> 00:28:59,880
And then you do the lovely thing of taking that spit

473
00:28:59,980 --> 00:29:02,760
and spit it in the bucket, and the open-air yeast

474
00:29:02,860 --> 00:29:04,560
would propagate, and you would basically

475
00:29:04,660 --> 00:29:06,960
have a fermented beverage.

476
00:29:07,060 --> 00:29:10,200
Sanke's earliest brewers were probably rice farmers,

477
00:29:10,201 --> 00:29:13,500
but production was soon taken over by the Shinto monks,

478
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:16,500
who usually controlled the village water supply.

479
00:29:16,600 --> 00:29:20,640
It's been closely tied to religion and ritual ever since.

480
00:29:20,740 --> 00:29:23,140
Some very, very happy people making sake.

481
00:29:23,240 --> 00:29:25,940
We can understand why.

482
00:29:26,040 --> 00:29:27,360
Welcome to Sake One.

483
00:29:27,460 --> 00:29:30,860
This is the only American-operated sake brewery,

484
00:29:30,960 --> 00:29:33,160
or we call it Kura, here in the US.

485
00:29:33,260 --> 00:29:34,560
There are a couple other breweries,

486
00:29:34,660 --> 00:29:36,600
but we're the only Americans who have approached it

487
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:38,900
at this level of brewing sake.

488
00:29:38,901 --> 00:29:42,740
Our entire focus here is making premium sake here in Oregon.

489
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:45,040
We established the brewery here in Forest Grove

490
00:29:45,140 --> 00:29:47,140
for one key reason, and that is the water

491
00:29:47,240 --> 00:29:48,740
that flows off the mountains here.

492
00:29:48,840 --> 00:29:50,420
It's a lot easier to get our water here

493
00:29:50,520 --> 00:29:51,640
than truck it around.

494
00:29:51,740 --> 00:29:54,420
Water is critical for the low iron and manganese

495
00:29:54,520 --> 00:29:57,120
that we're seeking that aid in the brewing process.

496
00:30:02,060 --> 00:30:04,600
If you'd rather skip the spitting in a bucket part,

497
00:30:04,700 --> 00:30:08,900
here's a quick tutorial on sake making.

498
00:30:08,901 --> 00:30:11,200
Get some rice.

499
00:30:11,300 --> 00:30:13,880
Mill the grain down to the starch-rich center.

500
00:30:13,980 --> 00:30:15,640
Soak it.

501
00:30:15,740 --> 00:30:18,180
Grow mold on it to break the starch molecules down

502
00:30:18,280 --> 00:30:20,340
into simple sugars.

503
00:30:20,440 --> 00:30:24,420
Feed that to some yeast, which will promptly excrete alcohol.

504
00:30:24,520 --> 00:30:27,280
And there you have it, sake.

505
00:30:27,380 --> 00:30:29,920
But the art is in the details.

506
00:30:30,020 --> 00:30:32,860
Sake is the industry of 10,000 methods.

507
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:35,060
That's the coined term.

508
00:30:35,160 --> 00:30:37,900
Everybody has their style of making sake.

509
00:30:37,901 --> 00:30:40,900
These guys fine-tune it year in and year out.

510
00:30:43,900 --> 00:30:45,900
Our mill is full, 4,000 pounds.

511
00:30:45,901 --> 00:30:51,900
It'll take about 17 to 18 hours to fully mill down to 58%.

512
00:30:51,901 --> 00:30:54,900
The amount of bran that is milled off the rice grain

513
00:30:54,901 --> 00:30:58,900
is one of the main factors in the quality of the resulting sake.

514
00:30:58,901 --> 00:31:02,900
So this is the first start of the mill right here at 90% milled.

515
00:31:02,901 --> 00:31:05,900
And then after our 18 hours,

516
00:31:05,901 --> 00:31:07,900
We have these little golden nuggets,

517
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:10,240
how small and how perfect they are.

518
00:31:10,340 --> 00:31:13,240
That's our moneymaker.

519
00:31:13,340 --> 00:31:16,340
Now for the money laundering.

520
00:31:16,440 --> 00:31:19,140
The rice goes through a bath to remove the fine coating

521
00:31:19,240 --> 00:31:21,040
of rice flour left by the milling.

522
00:31:26,960 --> 00:31:29,260
A vacuum tube carries it up to a soak tank.

523
00:31:33,400 --> 00:31:35,900
The water is drained off.

524
00:31:35,901 --> 00:31:42,900
Then down it goes, and up again to a vat to steam.

525
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:51,080
The steam will soften the rice and kill any wild bacteria

526
00:31:51,180 --> 00:31:52,020
or molds.

527
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:55,380
Right now, that rice is 180 degrees,

528
00:31:55,480 --> 00:31:57,520
and it will kill a mold spore.

529
00:31:57,620 --> 00:32:01,200
So he's now tipping the rice down into a hopper, which is

530
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:04,560
going to go down into a conveyor belt, which actually

531
00:32:04,561 --> 00:32:08,320
which actually has a drawdown fan, which will cool the rice

532
00:32:08,321 --> 00:32:09,860
as it goes up the belt.

533
00:32:13,320 --> 00:32:16,980
There's the mold spores right here coming up.

534
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:20,520
A simple fan blows a cloud of their proprietary mold spore

535
00:32:20,521 --> 00:32:21,740
onto the cooled rice.

536
00:32:22,180 --> 00:32:23,720
Now it's called koji.

537
00:32:24,060 --> 00:32:25,680
This is rice and mold.

538
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:28,480
Up the conveyor belt.

539
00:32:28,860 --> 00:32:31,220
A conveyor belt takes it to the first koji room,

540
00:32:31,640 --> 00:32:33,420
where the mold will have a day to germinate.

541
00:32:34,560 --> 00:32:38,560
The room must be as clean as possible, so only the right mold

542
00:32:38,660 --> 00:32:40,560
grows.

543
00:32:40,660 --> 00:32:44,000
The cedar paneling has natural antibiotic properties

544
00:32:44,100 --> 00:32:47,500
to reduce invasive molds, an ancient technique.

545
00:32:50,800 --> 00:32:54,520
After 24 hours, the mold spore has sprouted, and the rice

546
00:32:54,620 --> 00:32:56,020
has moved to the growing table.

547
00:32:56,021 --> 00:32:56,721
table.

548
00:33:02,520 --> 00:33:05,820
The koji mold will turn the rice into food for yeast.

549
00:33:14,880 --> 00:33:16,680
The yeast begins its feast.

550
00:33:21,880 --> 00:33:24,020
These vats will ferment for 18 days.

551
00:33:26,020 --> 00:33:29,260
It will be filtered, bottled, and shipped out,

552
00:33:29,360 --> 00:33:32,660
or you can have a taste of it fresh, right on site.

553
00:33:32,760 --> 00:33:35,760
And you don't need a little ceramic cup.

554
00:33:35,860 --> 00:33:38,760
There's specialty glassware now for sake,

555
00:33:38,860 --> 00:33:41,500
but we just grab a really nice white wine glass,

556
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:44,400
and that's a perfect place to start off with.

557
00:33:44,500 --> 00:33:46,480
Rice is delicious to drink,

558
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:48,540
but it has a serious side as well.

559
00:33:50,640 --> 00:33:52,640
Although it likes to grow in water,

560
00:33:52,740 --> 00:33:54,540
many countries that depend on rice

561
00:33:54,541 --> 00:33:57,380
on rice sometimes get too much water.

562
00:33:57,480 --> 00:34:01,280
Seasonal floods can devastate a rice field.

563
00:34:01,380 --> 00:34:04,580
Typically, a rice variety will survive underwater

564
00:34:04,680 --> 00:34:07,280
maybe three days, and if the flood lasts any longer,

565
00:34:07,380 --> 00:34:08,480
it will die.

566
00:34:08,580 --> 00:34:10,280
If their leaves are covered too long,

567
00:34:10,380 --> 00:34:13,000
the plant essentially drowns.

568
00:34:13,100 --> 00:34:16,900
In Bangladesh and India alone, four million tons of rice a year

569
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:22,300
are lost to flooding, enough to feed 30 million people.

570
00:34:22,301 --> 00:34:26,800
Pamela Ronald wanted to help get those 30 million fed.

571
00:34:26,900 --> 00:34:30,340
15 years ago, her team set out to make a variety of rice

572
00:34:30,440 --> 00:34:33,540
that could stand up to high flood water.

573
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:37,540
They already knew of one unpopular variety that could.

574
00:34:37,640 --> 00:34:40,360
Unlike virtually all of the varieties of rice,

575
00:34:40,460 --> 00:34:42,460
which die after three days of submergence,

576
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:45,060
this particular variety can withstand

577
00:34:45,160 --> 00:34:47,000
two weeks of submergence.

578
00:34:47,100 --> 00:34:50,160
But it tasted terrible and had a low yield.

579
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:52,060
Farmers didn't want it.

580
00:34:52,061 --> 00:34:55,160
Pam's team needed to find the gene that gave that unpopular

581
00:34:55,260 --> 00:34:59,600
variety its unique ability to withstand flooding.

582
00:34:59,700 --> 00:35:03,400
The gene isolation took about 10 years.

583
00:35:03,500 --> 00:35:07,240
We were able to pinpoint one region of the chromosome

584
00:35:07,340 --> 00:35:09,720
that had a very interesting gene.

585
00:35:09,820 --> 00:35:11,720
But was it the right one?

586
00:35:11,820 --> 00:35:15,220
To find out, they took the gene from the unpopular variety

587
00:35:15,320 --> 00:35:19,680
and stuck it into a bacterium that normally infects rice.

588
00:35:19,681 --> 00:35:21,680
Then they put the bacterium into the seeds

589
00:35:21,780 --> 00:35:24,280
of a different, tastier rice variety.

590
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:26,920
The bacterium acts like a shuttle service.

591
00:35:27,020 --> 00:35:30,320
It spliced its DNA with a copy of the important gene

592
00:35:30,420 --> 00:35:33,520
into the DNA of the tasty rice seeds.

593
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:35,420
Then they planted the seeds.

594
00:35:38,240 --> 00:35:42,040
So the new varieties behaved just like the old varieties,

595
00:35:42,140 --> 00:35:44,880
except when they were flooded, they would survive instead

596
00:35:44,980 --> 00:35:46,140
of die.

597
00:35:46,240 --> 00:35:48,480
They had found the waterproof gene.

598
00:35:48,481 --> 00:35:51,480
But that's only half the battle.

599
00:35:51,481 --> 00:35:54,480
Now to get it into a plant the farmers would accept

600
00:35:54,481 --> 00:35:56,480
and people would eat.

601
00:35:56,481 --> 00:35:58,480
And often genetically modified varieties

602
00:35:58,481 --> 00:36:00,480
are viewed with suspicion around the world.

603
00:36:02,480 --> 00:36:04,480
Pam passed her information about the gene

604
00:36:04,481 --> 00:36:07,480
to a research facility in Indonesia.

605
00:36:09,480 --> 00:36:12,480
They used Pam's information to breed a new variety

606
00:36:12,481 --> 00:36:15,480
using traditional cross-fertilization methods.

607
00:36:15,481 --> 00:36:17,380
fertilization methods.

608
00:36:17,480 --> 00:36:19,780
In the end, they produced a new rice plant

609
00:36:19,880 --> 00:36:24,720
with a waterproof ability without using genetic engineering,

610
00:36:24,820 --> 00:36:28,720
a good-tasting plant that everybody could accept.

611
00:36:28,820 --> 00:36:30,720
This emergence tolerance variety

612
00:36:30,820 --> 00:36:34,540
can survive underwater for up to 17 days.

613
00:36:34,640 --> 00:36:40,340
And that's usually sufficient for most flood conditions.

614
00:36:40,440 --> 00:36:42,040
News of the new rice is spreading

615
00:36:42,140 --> 00:36:45,080
in India and Indonesia, where losses due to flooding

616
00:36:45,081 --> 00:36:48,080
can be devastating.

617
00:36:48,180 --> 00:36:51,880
So if all those farms were growing

618
00:36:51,980 --> 00:36:55,120
submergence-tolerance rice, you'd be able to feed 30 million

619
00:36:55,220 --> 00:36:57,220
more people.

620
00:36:57,320 --> 00:37:00,120
Next, rice can show up anywhere.

621
00:37:00,220 --> 00:37:03,840
It might be in your beer or even your walls.

622
00:37:08,540 --> 00:37:10,480
Scientists have used genetic engineering

623
00:37:10,580 --> 00:37:14,340
to put a gene from the daffodil into a rice variety.

624
00:37:14,341 --> 00:37:17,040
The gene makes the rice rich in vitamin A.

625
00:37:17,720 --> 00:37:23,220
It may help the 500,000 children a year who go blind from vitamin A deficiency.

626
00:37:25,700 --> 00:37:30,120
The U.S. is a big exporter of rice, but most of it stays right here.

627
00:37:31,340 --> 00:37:35,700
The single largest rice buyer in America is Anheuser-Busch.

628
00:37:37,020 --> 00:37:40,360
About 10% of U.S. rice goes into beer.

629
00:37:40,361 --> 00:37:43,000
It's a very American style of brewing,

630
00:37:43,100 --> 00:37:44,600
and it makes the beer lighter tasting.

631
00:37:47,600 --> 00:37:49,800
The brewery in Placentia, California,

632
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:52,400
is one of the few so-called craft brewers

633
00:37:52,500 --> 00:37:55,500
that uses rice in a beer recipe.

634
00:37:55,600 --> 00:37:58,840
Craft brewers, by definition, brew less than 2 million

635
00:37:58,940 --> 00:38:00,320
barrels a year.

636
00:38:00,420 --> 00:38:03,820
Anheuser-Busch cranks out about 160 million.

637
00:38:03,920 --> 00:38:06,380
Rice started being used by the big breweries

638
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:10,360
because it was cheap, easy to eat,

639
00:38:10,361 --> 00:38:12,900
and it added the alcohol content

640
00:38:12,901 --> 00:38:14,900
while keeping the beer light.

641
00:38:14,901 --> 00:38:17,900
Virtually every beer has these four ingredients,

642
00:38:17,901 --> 00:38:23,900
water, barley, hops, and yeast.

643
00:38:23,901 --> 00:38:28,320
The brewery adds two more for their distinctive Belgian ale,

644
00:38:28,321 --> 00:38:32,320
Thai basil and rice.

645
00:38:32,321 --> 00:38:33,820
Cheers.

646
00:38:33,821 --> 00:38:36,220
So many people look down on it just because the macros

647
00:38:36,221 --> 00:38:38,820
brew with it, but it's really a great ingredient.

648
00:38:38,821 --> 00:38:42,420
It all starts with barley, milled to just slightly crush

649
00:38:42,520 --> 00:38:44,920
the grain.

650
00:38:45,020 --> 00:38:46,920
Mix that with hot water.

651
00:38:47,020 --> 00:38:51,560
That's what's going to be converted starch into sugar.

652
00:38:51,660 --> 00:38:55,340
That plus the rice, which goes in next,

653
00:38:55,440 --> 00:38:58,240
gelatinized to make the starch easily accessible.

654
00:39:01,540 --> 00:39:04,340
So what we're doing is we're adding a lot more starch

655
00:39:04,440 --> 00:39:06,940
to the mash, so the enzymes in the mash

656
00:39:06,941 --> 00:39:09,700
enzymes in mash can convert it in a sugar,

657
00:39:09,701 --> 00:39:14,440
which equals a higher alcohol beer for us.

658
00:39:14,441 --> 00:39:17,040
The two grains go together into a big vat of hot water.

659
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:23,340
The heat breaks the starch molecule into simple sugars.

660
00:39:31,540 --> 00:39:33,840
Then, a second rinse.

661
00:39:33,841 --> 00:39:36,340
What's happening right now is this is called sparging.

662
00:39:36,341 --> 00:39:42,420
And it's basically a hot water rinse of the grain to extract every last sugar we can.

663
00:39:42,421 --> 00:39:44,840
The resulting liquid looks sort of like beer.

664
00:39:44,841 --> 00:39:49,220
So you can see kind of what we're working with right here.

665
00:39:49,221 --> 00:39:55,100
So at this point it's just sweet water, sugar from the grain and water.

666
00:39:55,101 --> 00:40:01,020
It's called wort, no alcohol, but the sugar that's in this right now is going to end up

667
00:40:01,021 --> 00:40:05,460
being the alcohol when the yeast consumes it in the fermenter.

668
00:40:05,461 --> 00:40:07,960
The barley and the rice have done their job.

669
00:40:07,961 --> 00:40:10,460
Right now in the process, we are getting rid of what's

670
00:40:10,461 --> 00:40:11,460
called the spent grain.

671
00:40:11,461 --> 00:40:13,460
It's the grain that we've already used, extracted all

672
00:40:13,461 --> 00:40:14,460
the sugar we can from it.

673
00:40:14,461 --> 00:40:16,460
It still has some sugar left in it, probably about

674
00:40:16,461 --> 00:40:19,460
anywhere from 10 to 30 percent depending on the grain.

675
00:40:19,461 --> 00:40:21,460
So we have a farmer come and picks it up for free,

676
00:40:21,461 --> 00:40:22,460
and he gives it to his cattle.

677
00:40:22,461 --> 00:40:24,460
We don't have to pay for disposal, he doesn't have to

678
00:40:24,461 --> 00:40:26,460
pay for food, it's a great relationship.

679
00:40:35,460 --> 00:40:39,900
The sugar water, wort, heads to another vat for more boiling.

680
00:40:40,900 --> 00:40:44,400
It still has some solids in it, and it's about to get more.

681
00:40:45,540 --> 00:40:47,460
The Thai basil and the hops.

682
00:40:48,940 --> 00:40:51,300
Next up, we're going to transfer it over to the whirlpool,

683
00:40:51,500 --> 00:40:53,680
which we're going to separate the solids and the liquids

684
00:40:53,681 --> 00:40:56,120
before we knock it out to our fermenters.

685
00:40:57,200 --> 00:40:59,380
The fermentation will take about two weeks.

686
00:41:00,220 --> 00:41:02,020
The brewery is going against the grain.

687
00:41:02,021 --> 00:41:07,420
Brewing with rice for a Belgian-style beer with an Asian twist.

688
00:41:08,620 --> 00:41:16,360
Flavors we've noticed from using rice are coconut, tropical, maybe a slight pineapple.

689
00:41:16,920 --> 00:41:19,640
I try not to have any prejudices about any sort of ingredient.

690
00:41:20,040 --> 00:41:25,960
I think almost anything, well, not everything, but most things can taste pretty good in beer

691
00:41:25,961 --> 00:41:28,000
as long as it has some sort of fermentability.

692
00:41:28,001 --> 00:41:33,000
We've seen rice as the most versatile of grains,

693
00:41:33,100 --> 00:41:37,000
and that's just a small portion of the plant.

694
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:39,240
What happens to the rest of it?

695
00:41:39,340 --> 00:41:41,240
Rice straw.

696
00:41:41,340 --> 00:41:44,240
Well, for one thing, you can build a house with it,

697
00:41:44,340 --> 00:41:46,660
even if you're not a little pig.

698
00:41:46,760 --> 00:41:48,060
We get those jokes.

699
00:41:48,160 --> 00:41:50,160
I hope the big bad wolf doesn't come around and blow a dain.

700
00:41:50,260 --> 00:41:51,460
Other people, what?

701
00:41:51,560 --> 00:41:52,500
How did you do that?

702
00:41:52,600 --> 00:41:54,500
You know, they've never heard of the whole concept.

703
00:41:54,600 --> 00:41:58,000
Bruce and Robin Rominger did most of the work they did

704
00:41:58,001 --> 00:41:59,840
to build their rice house.

705
00:41:59,940 --> 00:42:02,000
This is the kind of bale we use right here.

706
00:42:02,100 --> 00:42:03,940
Produced only seven miles from here.

707
00:42:04,040 --> 00:42:06,100
After harvest, there's a lot of straw residue

708
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:08,140
left in the California rice field.

709
00:42:08,240 --> 00:42:10,740
They start with the wood frame construction.

710
00:42:10,840 --> 00:42:13,040
Then they bring in the bales.

711
00:42:13,140 --> 00:42:15,660
And you stack them like Legos.

712
00:42:15,760 --> 00:42:20,160
The bales fill all the spaces between the studs.

713
00:42:20,260 --> 00:42:21,860
They aren't used for support.

714
00:42:21,960 --> 00:42:28,000
It's basically insulation, really, really good insulation.

715
00:42:28,001 --> 00:42:31,000
They used over 200 bales to build three out of the four

716
00:42:31,001 --> 00:42:35,500
exterior walls for their 3,000-square-foot home.

717
00:42:35,501 --> 00:42:38,080
Well, it really is a straw house.

718
00:42:38,081 --> 00:42:39,880
And then the stucco goes right on it.

719
00:42:39,881 --> 00:42:42,080
So the stucco, there's three layers of stucco here

720
00:42:42,081 --> 00:42:44,420
that are stuck right to this straw.

721
00:42:44,421 --> 00:42:46,080
This is becoming more mainstream.

722
00:42:46,081 --> 00:42:49,660
There's a police headquarters built in Visalia out of straw

723
00:42:49,661 --> 00:42:50,660
bale.

724
00:42:50,661 --> 00:42:52,160
There's schools being built in California out of it.

725
00:42:52,161 --> 00:42:54,420
It's a very practical building material.

726
00:42:54,421 --> 00:42:57,160
I mean, that's obviously a cliche that it's a bunch of hippies

727
00:42:57,161 --> 00:42:58,160
of hippies doing this.

728
00:42:58,260 --> 00:42:59,600
But it's past that.

729
00:42:59,700 --> 00:43:02,900
But we cut our hair before you came, so.

730
00:43:03,000 --> 00:43:08,540
From houses to jewelry and 3 billion stomachs

731
00:43:08,640 --> 00:43:13,180
in between, rice fills an important place in our lives,

732
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:16,140
waves of grain for a hungry planet.

733
00:43:27,160 --> 00:43:29,220
you

